<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	RELEASE entitlements. The only difference from Coiny.entitlements is
	aps-environment, and that difference is the whole point of the file.

	TestFlight and App Store builds ALWAYS use the production APNs
	environment; there is no way to upload a TestFlight build that pushes
	against sandbox. A Release build carrying `development` registers a
	SANDBOX device token, the production backend sends to
	api.push.apple.com, and APNs answers 400 BadDeviceToken. Push is then
	silently dead on every TestFlight build, with no error anywhere the
	user or the server can see.

	This is exactly the pairing backend/src/push/apns.ts already reasons
	about. Its comment states the intended correspondence:

	    Debug -> staging -> development/sandbox
	    Release -> production -> production

	The backend half was correct. The app half was not: project.yml set
	`aps-environment: development` unconditionally, so the second line was
	false and the backend was choosing the right gateway for a token the
	app never issued.

	Hand-maintained rather than generated, because XcodeGen writes exactly
	one entitlements file per target and cannot vary it by configuration.
	CoinyTests/EntitlementsParityTests.swift fails if this file and
	Coiny.entitlements drift on anything other than aps-environment, so a
	capability added to project.yml cannot silently go missing from Release
	builds.
	-->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>production</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
